WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.480
On a rocky shore at Millport in Scotland, one barnacle lives high on the rock.

2
00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:12.100
Another covers the rock below.

3
00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:18.300
Joseph Connell removed the lower one, Balanus, from around the upper one,

4
00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:23.740
Chthamalus. Then Chthamalus survived well in the zone it normally lacks.

5
00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:25.860
What had held it back?

6
00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:31.200
Connell saw Balanus smother, undercut and crush its neighbour.

7
00:00:32.083 --> 00:00:33.723
That is competition.

8
00:00:34.983 --> 00:00:41.103
Individuals use a resource in short supply, so each lowers the survival, growth or

9
00:00:41.103 --> 00:00:42.483
reproduction of the others.

10
00:00:43.643 --> 00:00:48.063
The Lotka-Volterra model asks when two competitors can live together.

11
00:00:49.063 --> 00:00:55.703
Each species is slowed by its own numbers, up to its carrying capacity, K. It is

12
00:00:55.703 --> 00:00:59.383
also slowed by the other species, counted in its own units.

13
00:01:00.243 --> 00:01:05.923
α₁₂ is the effect of one species 2 individual on species 1.

14
00:01:06.883 --> 00:01:13.683
If α₁₂ is one half, two species 2 individuals crowd species 1 as much as

15
00:01:13.683 --> 00:01:14.683
one of its own.

16
00:01:15.217 --> 00:01:17.577
Take values invented for practice.

17
00:01:18.497 --> 00:01:22.757
K is 100 for species 1, and 80 for species 2.

18
00:01:23.537 --> 00:01:24.957
Both alphas are one half.

19
00:01:25.817 --> 00:01:30.297
With 20 of each, species 1 grows by 7 individuals per day.

20
00:01:31.257 --> 00:01:35.397
Now draw each isocline, the line where one species stops growing.

21
00:01:36.417 --> 00:01:41.297
Species 1 stops on a line from 100 on its own axis to 200 on the other.

22
00:01:42.017 --> 00:01:45.457
Species 2 stops on a line from 80 to 160.

23
00:01:46.277 --> 00:01:48.217
They cross at 80 and 40.

24
00:01:49.017 --> 00:01:50.677
Is that crossing stable?

25
00:01:51.677 --> 00:01:53.657
Let each species start rare.

26
00:01:54.617 --> 00:02:00.637
Species 1, rare among species 2 at its carrying capacity, grows by 0.3 per day.

27
00:02:01.457 --> 00:02:07.577
Species 2, rare among species 1 at 100, grows by 0.094 per day.

28
00:02:08.357 --> 00:02:09.937
Each can increase when rare.

29
00:02:10.577 --> 00:02:15.617
So in this sketch, the model predicts stable coexistence at 80 and 40.

30
00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:19.110
Now change one coefficient.

31
00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:22.330
Raise α₂₁ to 1.

32
00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:31.970
Species 2, rare among species 1 at 100, now has a growth rate of minus 0.0625 per

33
00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:36.150
day. It cannot get started, while species 1 still can.

34
00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:40.730
The model predicts that species 1 excludes species 2.

35
00:02:42.783 --> 00:02:45.483
Both outcomes came from two conditions.

36
00:02:46.323 --> 00:02:50.503
Species 1 needs K₁ above α₁₂K₂.

37
00:02:51.123 --> 00:02:55.623
Species 2 needs K₂ above α₂₁K₁.

38
00:02:56.443 --> 00:03:00.883
The growth rate never appears in them, so the fastest grower need not win.

39
00:03:01.883 --> 00:03:07.883
Keep the rule: in this model, competitors coexist when each limits itself more than

40
00:03:07.883 --> 00:03:08.663
it limits the other.
